From: Kernel Investigation <kinv@example.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sched/fair: defer avg_vruntime overflow WARN to avoid rq->lock
 self-deadlock via netconsole

The avg_vruntime overflow check in __sum_w_vruntime_add() runs under
rq->lock (IRQs disabled).  It is a plain WARN_ON_ONCE(), so when it
fires it printk()s synchronously.  On a host with a polling console
(netconsole/netpoll), vprintk_emit() -> console_unlock() flushes the
console inline:

  __enqueue_entity()                    [holds rq->lock]
    __sum_w_vruntime_add()
      WARN_ON_ONCE(...)
        printk -> console_unlock -> netconsole write_ext_msg
          -> netpoll_send_udp -> __netpoll_send_skb -> netpoll_poll_dev
            -> bnxt_poll_p5 -> bnxt_tx_int -> dev_consume_skb_any
              -> dev_kfree_skb_irq_reason (irqs_disabled() == true)
                -> raise_softirq_irqoff(NET_TX_SOFTIRQ)
                  -> wakeup_softirqd (!in_interrupt())
                    -> wake_up_process -> try_to_wake_up -> ttwu_queue
                      -> rq_lock(this rq)   <-- SAME rq->lock, DEADLOCK

The CPU spins forever on its own rq->lock.  Because that rq is then a
permanent "busiest" target, every idle CPU running (nohz) idle balance
convoys onto its rq->lock with IRQs disabled; those CPUs can no longer
answer TLB-shootdown / membarrier IPIs, so dozens more CPUs stall in
csd_lock_wait().  The NMI watchdog eventually declares a hard LOCKUP.

Observed on a 252-CPU AMD EPYC (Turin) host: WARN at fair.c:691 fired on
CPU 38 during execve()->sched_exec()->__schedule(); ~12s later CPU 3
panicked with "Hard LOCKUP" while spinning on CPU 38's rq->lock in
_nohz_idle_balance() -> sched_balance_rq().  52 CPUs were queued on that
one rq->lock and 60 more were stuck in csd_lock_wait().

The scheduler already knows warnings emitted under rq->lock must not
print inline: pick_next_entity() uses printk_deferred() for the
"pick_eevdf would have returned NULL" message, and core.c uses
printk_deferred() under rq->lock too.  This WARN was simply missed.

Wrap the WARN in printk_deferred_enter()/exit() so its console output is
deferred to a safe context (irq_work, after rq->lock is dropped) instead
of re-entering the scheduler through the console.  The warning still
fires and is logged; only the inline console flush (and thus the
try_to_wake_up() recursion) is avoided.  IRQs are always disabled here
(rq->lock is held), satisfying the printk_deferred requirement.

Signed-off-by: Kernel Investigation <kinv@example.com>
---
 kernel/sched/fair.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/sched/fair.c b/kernel/sched/fair.c
index 5dc612effbec..e8535d6b5a47 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/fair.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/fair.c
@@ -688,7 +688,20 @@ __sum_w_vruntime_add(struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq, struct sched_entity *se)
 	s64 w_vruntime, key = entity_key(cfs_rq, se);

 	w_vruntime = key * weight;
+	/*
+	 * This runs under rq->lock (IRQs disabled).  Emitting this warning must
+	 * not synchronously flush the consoles from here: with a polling console
+	 * such as netconsole, console_unlock() -> netpoll_poll_dev() cleans the
+	 * NIC Tx ring and frees skbs via dev_kfree_skb_irq() ->
+	 * raise_softirq_irqoff() -> wakeup_softirqd() -> try_to_wake_up(), which
+	 * takes this very same rq->lock and self-deadlocks the CPU (and, via the
+	 * idle-balance convoy on the now-stuck rq, the whole machine).  Defer the
+	 * console output like the other scheduler warnings emitted under rq->lock
+	 * (see pick_next_entity()).
+	 */
+	printk_deferred_enter();
 	WARN_ON_ONCE((w_vruntime >> 63) != (w_vruntime >> 62));
+	printk_deferred_exit();

 	cfs_rq->sum_w_vruntime += w_vruntime;
 	cfs_rq->sum_weight += weight;
--
